## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason with 128-bit data layout and perform blockwise rearrangements using only bitwise ops. It also checks handling of multi-word operands without branching.

## Key Concepts

- Block masking and extraction
- Cross-word recombination
- Branchless 128-bit manipulation

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 swapAdjacentBlocks128(uint128 x, unsigned block) {
    uint64_t M = (1ULL << block) - 1;
    uint64_t A0 = x.lo & M;
    uint64_t A1 = (x.lo >> block) & M;
    uint64_t A2 = x.hi & M;
    uint64_t A3 = (x.hi >> block) & M;
    uint64_t loP = (A0 << block) | A1;
    uint64_t hiP = (A2 << block) | A3;
    return (uint128){.hi = hiP, .lo = loP};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to non-uniform block counts or dynamic block that isn't divisor of 128?
- How to optimize for block=16 across many values in tight loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world bitfield extraction often needs to pull a contiguous block from a 128-bit value into the least-significant bits for parsing, with no branching for performance.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic using two 64-bit halves
- Bit masking and shifting across a 128-bit boundary
- Pure bitwise manipulations (no loops)

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractBitBlock128(uint128 x, unsigned start, unsigned width) {
  // Build mask in 128-bit form: width ones at LSBs
  uint128 M = {0,0};
  if (width == 128) { M.hi = ~0ULL; M.lo = ~0ULL; }
  else if (width < 64) { M.hi = 0; M.lo = (width==0)?0:((1ULL<<width)-1); }
  else { M.lo = ~0ULL; M.hi = (width-64==0)?0:((1ULL<<(width-64))-1); }
  // Shift right by start and apply mask
  // Implement 128-bit shift and AND here respecting the architecture
  uint128 y = {0,0};
  // (Assume helper shifts across hi/lo are available)
  y = shrl128(x, start);
  return and128(y, M);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for width exactly 64 or 128 in terms of branchless masks?
- How would you adapt to architectures lacking native 128-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to craft branchless 128-bit comparison, a common low-level operation in high-performance contexts.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless selection using masks
- 128-bit comparison via hi/lo parts
- Handling -1/0/1 outputs reliably

## Code Example

```c
int compare128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  int hi = (int)(a.hi > b.hi) - (int)(a.hi < b.hi);
  int lo = (int)(a.lo > b.lo) - (int)(a.lo < b.lo);
  int t  = (hi != 0);
  int m  = -t;
  return (hi & m) | (lo & ~m);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to unsigned 128-bit values?
- How would you extend to return an enum instead of int?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform multi-word arithmetic with pure bitwise/arith operations, handling carry without branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic via two 64-bit halves
- Carry propagation using unsigned overflow properties
- Branchless computation

## Code Example
```c
void add128(uint64_t a_hi, uint64_t a_lo, uint64_t b_hi, uint64_t b_lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  uint64_t lo = a_lo + b_lo;
  uint64_t carry = (lo < a_lo);
  uint64_t hi = a_hi + b_hi + carry;
  *out_hi = hi;
  *out_lo = lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256-bit operands?
- What are the potential pitfalls on platforms with different endianness?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests mastery of a classic bit-twiddling pattern to find the next permutation of a fixed-popcount bitset in constant time, without loops or branches. It also probes edge cases like overflow and zero input.

## Key Concepts

- Snoob algorithm for next higher with same popcount
- 64-bit overflow behavior and edge cases (n == 0 or max with k ones)
- Pure bitwise/shifts approach, no loops

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t nextWithSamePopcount(uint64_t n) {
  uint64_t smallest = n & -n;
  uint64_t ripple = n + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = n ^ ripple;
  return ripple | ((ones >> 2) / smallest);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary word size or signed integers?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a candidate’s ability to apply parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR) across multiple lanes in a 128-bit value, without loops, while extracting and packing results into specific lanes. It also probes attention to data layout and endianness in lane-oriented operations.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) for 32-bit lanes
- Bit masks and shifts to accumulate per-lane popcounts
- 128-bit data viewed as four 32-bit lanes and proper packing of results

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 perLanePopcount128(uint128 x) {
  auto count32 = [](uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v >> 8);
    v = v + (v >> 16);
    v = v + (v >> 32);
    return v & 0xFFULL;
  };

  uint64_t loCount  = count32(x.lo);
  uint64_t hiCount  = count32(x.hi);

  uint128 r = {0, 0};
  // lane0: lo low 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo
  r.lo = (loCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane1: lo high 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo at bits 32-39
  r.lo |= (loCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  // lane2: hi low 32 bits -> bits 64-71 of r.hi
  r.hi = (hiCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane3: hi high 32 bits -> bits 96-103 of r.hi
  r.hi |= (hiCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary lane widths (e.g., 64-bit lanes) while preserving parallelism?
- What are the potential pitfalls with endianness or portability across compilers for the approach above?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of compact bit-twiddling techniques to count bits without loops, a common interview staple for performance-critical code.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit counting (SIMD-style within a register)
- Bit masks and shifts for aggregation
- Portability across unsigned integers and compilers

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t n) {
  n = n - ((n >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (n * 0x01010101) >> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 64-bit?  
- What tests would you add to verify edge cases (all zeros, all ones, random patterns)?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 64-bit field layouts and perform cross-field moves without branches, a common pattern in graphics and encoding pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masking for 16-bit lanes
- Cross-lane permutation with shifts
- No loops/branches; constant-time operations

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t swapR_B(uint64_t v) {
  uint64_t r = v & 0x000000000000FFFFULL;
  uint64_t g = v & 0x00000000FFFF0000ULL;
  uint64_t b = v & 0x0000FFFF00000000ULL;
  uint64_t a = v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL;
  return (r << 32) | g | (b >> 32) | a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to swap G and A as well? 
- Analyze performance implications on 64-bit architectures with compiler optimizations.
